"Noise Stability of Transformer Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08287v1)\nciteturn31685view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31684view0\",\"lineno\":1139}); Total lines: 1225\nL1113: ### I.2 Stability Propagation through a single Attention Layer\nL1114: \nL1115: For convenience, the following result defines a stability matrix, which captures the correlation of each input token with the other. Assumming all the entries in that matrix are bounded in some interval, we analyze the noise stability propagation through an attention layer. We also assume some structural properties of the weight matrices to allow for stability propagation in our proof:\nL1116: ###### Lemma 12 (Stability Propagation through an Attention Layer).\nL1117: Let $X,Y$ have stability matrix $\\{C\\}_{k\\ell,k^{\\prime}\\ell^{\\prime}}=\\mathbb{E}[X_{k\\ell}Y_{k^{\\prime}\\ell^{\\prime}}]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{nd\\times nd}$ with $0<\\rho_{\\ell}\\leq C_{k\\ell,k^{\\prime}\\ell^{\\prime}}\\leq\\rho_{r}$ for all $k,k^{\\prime}\\in[n],\\ell,\\ell^{\\prime}\\in[d]$, and suppose $||X||_{\\infty},||Y||_{\\infty}\\leq B$ with probability $1$. Consider an attention layer with matrices $W_{K},W_{Q},W_{V}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d}$ and denote, for two vectors $x,y\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ the following quantities:\nL1118:  | $$S^{+}(x,y)=\\sum\\limits_{i,j\\in[d]}\\max(0,x_{i}y_{j}),\\quad S^{-}(x,y)=\\sum\\limits_{i,j\\in[d]}\\min(0,x_{i}y_{j})$$  |\nL1119: \nL1120: Suppose that $W_{V}$ is such that $\\rho_{\\ell}S^{+}(w_{j},w_{j^{\\prime}})+\\rho_{r}S^{-}(w_{j},w_{j^{\\prime}})>0$ for all $j,j^{\\prime}\\in[d]$, where $w_{j}=(W_{V})_{:,j}$ is the $j$-th column of $W_{V}$. Hence, let:\nL1121:  | $\\displaystyle R_{\\ell}$  | $\\displaystyle:=\\inf\\limits_{j,j^{\\prime}\\in[d]}\\{\\rho_{\\ell}S^{+}(w_{j},w_{j^{\\prime}})+\\rho_{r}S^{-}(w_{j},w_{j^{\\prime}})\\}>0$  |\nL1122:  | $\\displaystyle R_{r}$  | $\\displaystyle:=\\sup\\limits_{j,j^{\\prime}\\in[d]}\\{\\rho_{r}S^{+}(w_{j},w_{j^{\\prime}})\\}$  |\nL1123: \nL1124: Now, let $A(X)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times d}$ be the output of the attention layer. Then we have that:\nL1125: \nL1126:  | $\\displaystyle 0<\\frac{R_{\\ell}}{E}\\leq\\mathbb{E}(A(X)_{ij}A(Y)_{i^{\\prime}j^{\\prime}})\\leq R_{r}\\cdot E$  |  | (9)\nL1127: for all $i,i^{\\prime}\\in[n],j,j^{\\prime}\\in[d]$, where $E:=\\exp(4d^{2}B^{2}||W_{K}||_{\\infty}||W_{Q}||_{\\infty})$\nL1128: ###### Proof.\nL1129: \nL1130: Let $S^{X}=\\sigma(XW_{Q}W_{K}^{T}X^{T})$ and $V^{X}:=XW_{V}$. We have that $A(X)_{ij}=\\langle S_{i,:}^{X},V_{:,j}^{X}\\rangle$, so:\nL1131:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}(A(X)_{ij}\\cdot A(Y)_{i^{\\prime}j^{\\prime}})$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\langle S_{i,:}^{X},V_{:,j}^{X}\\rangle\\cdot\\langle S_{i,:}^{Y},V_{:,j}^{Y}\\rangle\\right]$  |\nL1132:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\left(\\sum\\limits_{k=1}^{n}S_{ik}^{X}V_{kj}^{X}\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\sum\\limits_{k^{\\prime}=1}^{n}S_{i^{\\prime}k^{\\prime}}^{Y}V_{k^{\\prime}j^{\\prime}}^{Y}\\right)\\right]$  |\nL1133:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\sum\\limits_{k,k^{\\prime}}S_{ik}^{X}S_{i^{\\prime};k^{\\prime}}^{Y}V_{kj}^{X}V_{k^{\\prime}j^{\\prime}}^{Y}\\right]$  |\nL1134: Now let $q_{i}^{X}:=X_{i,:}\\cdot W_{Q}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$, $k_{i}^{X}=X_{i,:}\\cdot W_{K}$. We have:\nL1135: \nL1136:  | $$S_{ik}^{X}=\\frac{\\exp(q_{i}^{X}\\cdot k_{k})}{\\sum\\limits_{s=1}^{n}\\exp(q_{i}^{X}\\cdot k_{s})}$$  |\nL1137: \nL1138: Therefore:\nL1139:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}(A(X)_{ij}\\cdot A(Y)_{i^{\\prime}j^{\\prime}})$  |\nL1140:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\sum\\limits_{k,k^{\\prime}}\\frac{\\exp(q_{i}^{X}k_{k})}{\\sum\\limits_{s=1}^{n}\\exp(q_{i}^{X}k_{s})}\\cdot\\frac{\\exp(q_{i^{\\prime}}^{Y}k_{k^{\\prime}})}{\\sum\\limits_{s=1}^{n}\\exp(q_{i^{\\prime}}^{Y}k_{s})}\\cdot V_{kj}^{X}V_{k^{\\prime}j^{\\prime}}^{Y}\\right]$  |\nL1141:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\sum\\limits_{k,k^{\\prime}}\\frac{\\exp(X_{i,:}W_{Q}W_{K}^{T}X_{k,:}^{T})}{\\sum\\limits_{s=1}^{n}\\exp(X_{i,:}W_{Q}W_{K}^{T}X_{s,:}^{T})}\\cdot\\frac{\\exp(Y_{i^{\\prime},:}W_{Q}W_{K}^{T}Y_{k^{\\prime},:}^{T})}{\\sum\\limits_{s=1}^{n}\\exp(Y_{i^{\\prime},:}W_{Q}W_{K}^{T}Y_{s,:}^{T})}\\cdot X_{k,:}(W_{V})_{:,j}Y_{k^{\\prime},:}(W_{V})_{:,j^{\\prime}}\\right]$  |\nL1142: We will bound the softmax terms using the norms of $W_{Q},W_{K}$ and $X$. For any $s_{1},s_{2}$ we have:\nL1143: \nL1144:  | $\\displaystyle|X_{s_{1},:}W_{Q}W_{K}^{T}X_{s_{2},:}^{T}|\\leq d^{2}B^{2}||W_{Q}||_{\\infty}\\cdot||W_{K}||_{\\infty}$  |\nL1145: \nL1146: And this implies that:\nL1147:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{n}\\exp(-2d^{2}B^{2}||W_{K}||_{\\infty}||W_{Q}||_{\\infty})\\leq\\frac{\\exp(X_{i,:}W_{Q}W_{K}^{T}X_{k,:}^{T})}{\\sum\\limits_{s=1}^{n}\\exp(X_{i,:}W_{Q}W_{K}^{T}X_{s,:}^{T})}\\leq\\frac{1}{n}\\exp(2d^{2}B^{2}||W_{K}||_{\\infty}||W_{Q}||_{\\infty})$  |\nL1148: \nL1149: We now analyze the isolated terms\nL1150: \nL1151:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}[X_{k,:}(W_{V})_{:,j}Y_{k^{\\prime},:}(W_{V})_{:,j}]$  |\nL1152: \nL1153: We can use our prior information on the stability matrix $C$. Fix some $k,k^{\\prime}$ and we have that:\nL1154:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}[X_{k,:}(W_{V})_{:,j}Y_{k^{\\prime},:}(W_{V})_{:,j}]$  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum\\limits_{\\ell,\\ell^{\\prime}}\\mathbb{E}\\left[X_{k\\ell}(W_{V})_{\\ell j}Y_{k^{\\prime}\\ell^{\\prime}}(W_{V})_{\\ell^{\\prime}j^{\\prime}}\\right]$  |\nL1155:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum\\limits_{\\ell,\\ell^{\\prime}}(W_{V})_{\\ell j}(W_{V})_{\\ell^{\\prime}j^{\\prime}}\\cdot\\mathbb{E}\\left[X_{k\\ell}Y_{k^{\\prime}\\ell^{\\prime}}\\right]$  |\nL1156:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\in[R_{\\ell},R_{r}]$  |  | (as $\\mathbb{E}\\left[X_{k\\ell}Y_{k^{\\prime}\\ell^{\\prime}}\\right]\\in(r_{\\ell},r_{r})$)\nL1157: Overall, if we let $E:=\\exp(4d^{2}B^{2}||W_{K}||_{\\infty}||W_{Q}||_{\\infty})$, we get that:\nL1158: \nL1159:  | $\\displaystyle 0<\\frac{R_{\\ell}}{E}\\leq\\mathbb{E}(A(X)_{ij}\\cdot A(Y)_{i^{\\prime}j^{\\prime}})\\leq R_{r}\\cdot E$  |\nL1160: \nL1161: ∎\nL1162: ## Appendix J Noise Stability Regularization Experiments\nL1163: ### J.1 Architecture Layout, Hyperparameters and Training Details\nL1164: We present details of our architecture, training and hyperparameters. These can also be found in our codebase. Each Transformer layer uses multi-head self-attention followed by a position-wise feed-forward network, with residual connections around both sublayers. We use sinusoidal positional encodings, as well as binary attention masking $M$. We also use dropout, applied to attention weights, attention output, and FFN hidden layer with rate $p$. The activation function we use for our FFN is ReLU.\nL1165: To produce a classification label we use mean pooling over the label dimension.\nL1166: For initialization, linear layers are initialized with $\\mathcal{N}(0,0.02^{2})$ and zero biases. Every other learnable parameter is initialized via Xavier initialization. Positional encodings are fixed and not trainable.\nL1167: For training, we use AdamW with learning rate $\\eta$ and $\\ell_{2}$ weight decay regularization $\\lambda$. Our loss is the cross entropy loss. We use a learning rate scheduler that reduces $\\eta$ on validation loss plateau. The patience and factor parameters of the scheduler are hyperparameters we set. Our codebase also provides support for multi-GPU and distributed training, though our models were too small to benefit from such augmentations.\nL1168: Table 2: Model Hyperparameters and Training Configuration for Modular Addition\nL1169: Name  | Symbol  | Default\nL1170: --- | --- | ---\nL1171: Embedding dimension  | $d_{\\text{model}}$  | 128\nL1172: Transformer layers  | $L$  | 2\nL1173: Attention heads  | $H$  | 2\nL1174: Max seq length (PE)  | max_length  | 512\nL1175: Vocab size  | $|\\mathcal{V}|$  | $K+5$\nL1176: Num classes  | $C$  | $K=113$\nL1177: Dropout rate  | $p$  | 0.1\nL1178: Batch size  | $B$  | 256\nL1179: Epochs  | $T$  | 7000\nL1180: Learning rate  | $\\eta$  | 0.001\nL1181: Weight decay  | $\\lambda$  | 0.001\nL1182: Label smoothing  | –  | 0.0\nL1183: Scheduler patience  | –  | 10 epochs\nL1184: Scheduler factor  | –  | 0.8\nL1185: Train samples  | –  | 2000\nL1186: Val/Test samples  | –  | 200 / 200\nL1187: ### J.2 Evolution of Noise Stability During Training.\nL1188: \nL1189: We also analyze how noise stability (with $\\rho=1/2$) evolves during training without regularization, focusing on the noisy sparse parity (NSP) task first because its target function has a known, low noise stability of $\\rho^{k}$ (cite69†O’Donnell, 2021 ). We make three key observations:\nL1190: \nL1191:   * •\nL1192: A randomly initialized Transformer exhibits high noise stability, which decreases throughout training to converge toward the low stability level of the target function (cite184†Figure 11 ).\nL1193: \nL1194:   * •\nL1195: \nL1196: Noise stability is a leading indicator for generalization. cite184†Figure 11 shows that stability begins to decrease well before the sharp drop in validation loss, signaling that internal model adjustments precede performance improvements.\nL1197: \nL1198:   * •\nL1199: Stability can serve as a secondary metric for model selection. Among models with similar validation loss, the one whose stability best aligns with the theoretical properties of the target function may be preferable.\nL1200: \nL1201: cite185†Image: Refer to caption Figure 11: Evolution of noise stability and validation loss for noisy sparse parity. Stability (green) begins to decrease before the validation loss (blue) drops, acting as a leading indicator for generalization.\nL1202: ### J.3 Experiments on Language Generation\nL1203: We also tested noise stability regularization on a language generation task. We trained a 4 layer transformer model with $d_{\\text{model}}=30$ and $H=6$ on the next-token-prediction task. The dataset we used was WikiText-2-v1 (Small), with sequence length $N=20$, a vocabulary size of $500$, batch size of $200$ and $1000$ training examples^{8}^{8} 8 Our setup can also be found in full in our codebase..\nL1204: We trained our transformer without noise stability regularization, with weight decay $\\lambda=0.02$ and with numerous settings of $(\\rho,\\gamma)$. We tracked noise stability, validation loss and validation accuracy throughout training.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNoise Stability of Transformer Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08287v1)\nciteturn31685view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31684view0\",\"lineno\":715}); Total lines: 1225\nL660: ### C.4 From OU Theory to Stability\nL661: \nL662: We are finally ready to define stability in familiar terms:\nL663: ###### Definition C.8 (Gaussian Noise Stability).\nL664: \nL665: Let $f\\in L^{2}(\\gamma)$ and $X\\sim\\gamma^{n}$ where $\\gamma\\equiv\\mathcal{N}(0,1)$. Let $\\rho\\in(0,1)$ and $Y=\\rho X+Z\\sqrt{1-\\rho^{2}}$ for $Z\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I_{n})$ independent from $X$. We define the stability of $f$ as:\nL666: \nL667:  | $$\\text{Stab}_{\\rho}(f):=\\mathop{\\mathbb{E}}\\limits_{(X,Y)}[f(X)f(Y)]$$  |\nL668: \nL669: We note the immediate connection between stability and the OU semigroup:\nL670: \nL671:  | $$\\text{Stab}_{\\rho}(f)=\\langle T_{\\rho}f,f\\rangle_{L^{2}(\\gamma)}$$  |\nL672: Now we know from the spectral expansion of the OU Semigroup action that:\nL673: \nL674:  | $$T_{\\rho}f=\\sum\\limits_{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{N}^{d}}\\rho^{|\\alpha|}\\hat{f}(\\alpha)H_{\\alpha}$$  |\nL675: \nL676: By orthogonality of the Hermite polynomials we have:\nL677:  | $$\\text{Stab}_{\\rho}(f)=\\left\\langle\\sum\\limits_{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{N}^{d}}\\rho^{|\\alpha|}\\hat{f}(\\alpha)H_{\\alpha},\\sum\\limits_{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{N}^{d}}\\rho^{|\\alpha|}\\hat{f}(\\alpha)H_{\\alpha}\\right\\rangle=\\sum\\limits_{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{N}^{d}}\\rho^{|\\alpha|}2^{|\\alpha|}\\cdot\\alpha!\\cdot\\hat{f}(\\alpha)^{2}$$  |\nL678: \nL679: If we define $\\tilde{f}(a)=\\sqrt{2^{|\\alpha|}{\\alpha!}}\\hat{f}(\\alpha)$ we finally get:\nL680:  | $\\displaystyle\\text{Stab}_{\\rho}(f)=\\sum\\limits_{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{N}^{d}}\\rho^{|\\alpha|}\\tilde{f}(\\alpha)^{2}$  |\nL681: ## Appendix D Proof of cite178†Lemma 1 L682: ###### Lemma 6 (Spectral tail bound via stability).\nL683: \nL684: Let $f\\in L^{2}(\\gamma^{n})$ be a square-integrable function under the standard Gaussian measure $\\gamma^{n}$. Suppose that\nL685: \nL686:  | $$\\text{Stab}_{\\rho}(f):=\\mathbb{E}[f(X)f(Y)]\\geq(1-\\delta)||f||_{2}^{2}$$  |\nL687: \nL688: for some $\\rho\\in(0,1)$ and $0<\\delta<\\varepsilon<1$, where $(X,Y)$ are standard Gaussian vectors with correlation $\\rho$, i.e., $Y=\\rho X+\\sqrt{1-\\rho^{2}}Z$ for $Z\\sim\\gamma^{n}$ independent of $X$.\nL689: \nL690: Then $f$ is $(\\varepsilon,T)$-concentrated for\nL691:  | $$\\boxed{T\\geq\\frac{\\log\\left(1-\\delta/\\varepsilon\\right)}{\\log\\rho}}$$  |\nL692: \nL693: in the sense that:\nL694: \nL695:  | $$\\sum_{k\\geq T}\\sum_{|\\alpha|=k}\\hat{f}(\\alpha)^{2}\\cdot 2^{|\\alpha|}\\alpha!\\leq\\varepsilon||f||_{2}^{2}$$  |\nL696: \nL697: where $f(x)=\\sum_{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{N}^{n}}\\hat{f}(\\alpha)H_{\\alpha}(x)$ is the expansion of $f$ in the multivariate physicists’ Hermite polynomial basis.\nL698: ###### Proof.\nL699: \nL700: Let us write the Hermite expansion of $f$ as\nL701: \nL702:  | $$f(x)=\\sum_{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{N}^{n}}\\hat{f}(\\alpha)H_{\\alpha}(x)$$  |\nL703: \nL704: where $H_{\\alpha}(x)=\\prod_{i=1}^{n}H_{\\alpha_{i}}(x_{i})$, and $H_{k}$ is the physicists’ Hermite polynomial of degree $k$. The Hermite polynomials are orthogonal in $L^{2}(\\gamma^{n})$ with squared norm $\\|H_{\\alpha}\\|^{2}=2^{|\\alpha|}\\alpha!$.\nL705: \nL706: Parseval’s identity in this basis reads:\nL707:  | $$\\left\\lVert f\\right\\rVert_{2}^{2}=\\sum_{\\alpha}\\hat{f}(\\alpha)^{2}\\cdot 2^{|\\alpha|}\\alpha!$$  |\nL708: \nL709: We define the Hermite level-$k$ weight of $f$ as:\nL710: \nL711:  | $$W^{(k)}[f](f):=\\sum_{|\\alpha|=k}\\hat{f}(\\alpha)^{2}\\cdot 2^{|\\alpha|}\\alpha!$$  |\nL712: \nL713: With this notation, Parseval’s identity is simply $\\left\\lVert f\\right\\rVert_{2}^{2}=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}W^{(k)}[f](f)$.\nL714: \nL715: The Ornstein–Uhlenbeck semigroup $T_{\\rho}$ acts on the Hermite expansion by\nL716:  | $$T_{\\rho}f(x)=\\sum_{\\alpha}\\rho^{|\\alpha|}\\hat{f}(\\alpha)H_{\\alpha}(x)$$  |\nL717: \nL718: The noise stability can therefore be written as:\nL719: \nL720:  | $\\displaystyle\\text{Stab}_{\\rho}(f)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\left\\langle f,T_{\\rho}f\\right\\rangle$  |\nL721:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{\\alpha}\\rho^{|\\alpha|}\\hat{f}(\\alpha)^{2}\\cdot 2^{|\\alpha|}\\alpha!$  |\nL722:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\rho^{k}W^{(k)}[f](f)$  |\nL723: \nL724: Fix a threshold $T\\in\\mathbb{N}$. We can split the sum at degree $T$:\nL725:  | $$\\text{Stab}_{\\rho}(f)=\\sum_{k<T}\\rho^{k}W^{(k)}[f](f)+\\sum_{k\\geq T}\\rho^{k}W^{(k)}[f](f)$$  |\nL726: \nL727: Since $\\rho\\in(0,1)$, we can upper-bound the terms in the sums by $\\rho^{k}\\leq 1$ for $k<T$ and $\\rho^{k}\\leq\\rho^{T}$ for $k\\geq T$. This gives:\nL728:  | $\\displaystyle\\text{Stab}_{\\rho}(f)$  | $\\displaystyle\\leq\\sum_{k<T}W^{(k)}[f](f)+\\rho^{T}\\sum_{k\\geq T}W^{(k)}[f](f)$  |\nL729:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\left(\\left\\lVert f\\right\\rVert_{2}^{2}-\\sum_{k\\geq T}W^{(k)}[f](f)\\right)+\\rho^{T}\\sum_{k\\geq T}W^{(k)}[f](f)$  |\nL730:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\left\\lVert f\\right\\rVert_{2}^{2}-(1-\\rho^{T})\\sum_{k\\geq T}W^{(k)}[f](f)$  |\nL731: \nL732: Rearranging the inequality to isolate the tail sum, we have:\nL733:  | $$\\sum_{k\\geq T}W^{(k)}[f](f)\\leq\\frac{\\left\\lVert f\\right\\rVert_{2}^{2}-\\text{Stab}_{\\rho}(f)}{1-\\rho^{T}}$$  |\nL734: \nL735: Using the assumption that $\\text{Stab}_{\\rho}(f)\\geq(1-\\delta)\\left\\lVert f\\right\\rVert_{2}^{2}$, we can further bound the numerator:\nL736: \nL737:  | $$\\sum_{k\\geq T}W^{(k)}[f](f)\\leq\\frac{\\left\\lVert f\\right\\rVert_{2}^{2}-(1-\\delta)\\left\\lVert f\\right\\rVert_{2}^{2}}{1-\\rho^{T}}=\\frac{\\delta\\left\\lVert f\\right\\rVert_{2}^{2}}{1-\\rho^{T}}$$  |\nL738: To ensure this tail sum is at most $\\varepsilon\\left\\lVert f\\right\\rVert_{2}^{2}$, it suffices to have:\nL739: \nL740:  | $$\\frac{\\delta\\left\\lVert f\\right\\rVert_{2}^{2}}{1-\\rho^{T}}\\leq\\varepsilon\\left\\lVert f\\right\\rVert_{2}^{2}$$  |\nL741: \nL742: Assuming $f$ is not the zero function, we can cancel $\\left\\lVert f\\right\\rVert_{2}^{2}$ and solve for $T$:\nL743: \nL744:  | $$T\\log\\rho\\leq\\log\\left(1-\\frac{\\delta}{\\varepsilon}\\right)$$  |\nL745:  | $$T\\geq\\frac{\\log\\left(1-\\delta/\\varepsilon\\right)}{\\log\\rho}$$  |\nL746: This bound is well-defined provided the argument of the logarithm is positive, which holds due to the assumption that $\\delta<\\varepsilon$. ∎\nL747: ## Appendix E Proof of cite123†Theorem 5.1 L748: \nL749: ###### Theorem E.1 (Stability of ReLU).\nL750: \nL751: Let $(X,Y)\\sim\\mathcal{N}\\left(0,\\begin{bmatrix}1&\\rho\\\\\nL752: \\rho&1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)$ for $\\rho\\in(-1,1)$. Then:\nL753: \nL754:  | $$\\mathbb{E}[\\text{ReLU}(X)\\text{ReLU}(Y)]=\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\left(\\sqrt{1-\\rho^{2}}+\\rho(\\pi-\\arccos\\rho)\\right)$$  |\nL755: ###### Proof.\nL756: \nL757: We compute the expectation:\nL758: \nL759:  | $\\displaystyle I(\\rho):=\\mathbb{E}[\\text{ReLU}(X)\\text{ReLU}(Y)]=\\mathbb{E}[\\max(0,X)\\max(0,Y)].$  |\nL760: \nL761: The joint density function of $(X,Y)$ is\nL762: \nL763:  | $$f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{1-\\rho^{2}}}\\exp\\left(-\\frac{x^{2}-2\\rho xy+y^{2}}{2(1-\\rho^{2})}\\right).$$  |\nL764: \nL765: Thus, we can compute:\nL766: \nL767:  | $$I(\\rho)=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\int_{0}^{\\infty}xy\\cdot f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx.$$  |\nL768: We perform a change to polar coordinates $x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad r\\in[0,\\infty),\\theta\\in[0,\\pi/2]$ so that $x,y\\geq 0$. Then the Jacobian is $dx\\,dy=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$ and the exponent becomes:\nL769:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{x^{2}-2\\rho xy+y^{2}}{2(1-\\rho^{2})}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{r^{2}(\\cos^{2}\\theta-2\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta+\\sin^{2}\\theta)}{2(1-\\rho^{2})}$  |  | (Substitute polar coordinates)\nL770:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{r^{2}(1-\\rho\\sin(2\\theta))}{2(1-\\rho^{2})}$  |  | ($\\sin(2\\theta)=2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$)\nL771: \nL772: So the integral becomes:\nL773:  | $\\displaystyle I(\\rho)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{\\infty}r^{2}\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\cdot\\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{1-\\rho^{2}}}\\exp\\left(-\\frac{r^{2}(1-\\rho\\sin(2\\theta))}{2(1-\\rho^{2})}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$  |\nL774:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{1-\\rho^{2}}}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\int_{0}^{\\infty}r^{3}\\exp\\left(-\\frac{r^{2}(1-\\rho\\sin(2\\theta))}{2(1-\\rho^{2})}\\right)dr\\,d\\theta.$  |\nL775: \nL776: Now let\nL777: \nL778:  | $$a(\\theta)=\\frac{1-\\rho\\sin(2\\theta)}{2(1-\\rho^{2})}.$$  |\nL779: Use the substitution $u=r^{2}\\Rightarrow du=2r\\,dr$, so:\nL780: \nL781:  | $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\infty}r^{3}e^{-a(\\theta)r^{2}}\\,dr$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{\\infty}ue^{-a(\\theta)u}\\,du=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{a(\\theta)^{2}}$  |  | (Standard integral: $\\int_{0}^{\\infty}ue^{-au}du=\\frac{1}{a^{2}}$)\nL782: \nL783: So we obtain:\nL784:  | $\\displaystyle I(\\rho)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{1-\\rho^{2}}}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\cdot\\frac{1}{2a(\\theta)^{2}}\\,d\\theta$  |\nL785:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\sqrt{1-\\rho^{2}}}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\frac{\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta}{a(\\theta)^{2}}\\,d\\theta.$  |\nL786: \nL787: Now recall that:\nL788: \nL789:  | $\\displaystyle a(\\theta)=\\frac{1-\\rho\\sin(2\\theta)}{2(1-\\rho^{2})}\\Rightarrow a(\\theta)^{2}=\\frac{(1-\\rho\\sin(2\\theta))^{2}}{4(1-\\rho^{2})^{2}}$  |\nL790: \nL791: So:\nL792:  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{a(\\theta)^{2}}=\\frac{4(1-\\rho^{2})^{2}}{(1-\\rho\\sin(2\\theta))^{2}}$  |\nL793: \nL794: Plugging in:\nL795: \nL796:  | $\\displaystyle I(\\rho)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\sqrt{1-\\rho^{2}}}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\cdot\\frac{4(1-\\rho^{2})^{2}}{(1-\\rho\\sin(2\\theta))^{2}}\\,d\\theta$  |\nL797:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{(1-\\rho^{2})^{3/2}}{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\frac{\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta}{(1-\\rho\\sin(2\\theta))^{2}}\\,d\\theta.$  |\nL798: \nL799: Now make the substitution $u=\\tan\\theta$, so:\nL800:  | $$\\sin(2\\theta)=\\frac{2u}{1+u^{2}},\\quad\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta=\\frac{u}{(1+u^{2})^{2}}\\,du$$  |\nL801: \nL802: Thus:\nL803: \nL804:  | $$\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\frac{\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta}{(1-\\rho\\sin(2\\theta))^{2}}\\,d\\theta=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\frac{u}{(1+u^{2})^{2}\\left(1-\\rho\\cdot\\frac{2u}{1+u^{2}}\\right)^{2}}\\,du=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\frac{u}{\\left(u^{2}+1-2\\rho u\\right)^{2}}\\,du.$$  |\nL805: \nL806: This integral can be evaluated by completing the square:\nL807: \nL808:  | $$u^{2}-2\\rho u+1=(u-\\rho)^{2}+(1-\\rho^{2})$$  |\nL809: \nL810: So, now consider the integral\nL811:  | $$I:=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\frac{u}{\\big((u-\\rho)^{2}+(1-\\rho^{2})\\big)^{2}}\\,du,$$  |\nL812: \nL813: where we define\nL814: \nL815:  | $$a:=\\sqrt{1-\\rho^{2}}.$$  |\nL816: \nL817: We rewrite the numerator as\nL818: \nL819:  | $\\displaystyle I$  | $\\displaystyle=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(u-\\rho)+\\rho}{\\big((u-\\rho)^{2}+a^{2}\\big)^{2}}\\,du$  |\n"
